\chapter{On1x and MuLIB}
\label{ch:1}
\index{On1x and MuLIB}

On1x is a statically compiled, ML-influenced language with imperative control and a C11 reference backend. It supplies the source language, compiler, libraries, and tools. MuLIB supplies reusable C11 kernels. LibStone supplies optional generators, analysis engines, and code-generation tools. An application can use On1x arithmetic and functions without calling a MuLIB kernel. A C program can use many MuLIB kernels without involving the On1x compiler at all.

\section{Follow one expression}
In \OnxCodeTerm{println(40 + 2)}, the scanner identifies tokens, the parser forms the call and addition, and the checker resolves the operation to a concrete integer type. The compiler can simplify the checked addition before emitting C. The resulting program still needs an implementation of the printing intrinsic. A kernel-backed call adds more questions about signatures, implementation placement, and linkage. \cref{ch:4} traces the build and \cref{ch:91} explains the interop boundary.

Capability claims in this book refer to the repository snapshot and the named execution profile. For a feature you plan to ship, identify the source that accepts it, the backend that lowers it, and a test that executes it or rejects an invalid case. A syntax note or catalogue entry is only one piece of that evidence.

\section{Does a buffer make a string?}
Suppose a C kernel reports that its output occupies three bytes. They are
the letter A, a zero, and the letter B, in that order. The length is useful
information: a consumer of bytes can read all three. A consumer that looks
for the first zero to find the end of a C string sees just the A. Both
consumers can be handed the same address. They will give different answers
to the question ``how much data is here?''

Work backwards from a proposed On1x wrapper returning a string. It must
either establish that the data meet the string contract or perform an
explicit conversion with a specified meaning. Copying three bytes to a new
allocation would solve a storage lifetime problem, but would still leave the
zero in the middle. If the kernel is meant to deliver arbitrary bytes, the
natural result preserves the length and the zero. \Cref{ch:7} shows how to
inspect such a result without depending on console rendering.

Now ask who may destroy the original storage. If the address was a borrowed
view into a kernel owner, the wrapper cannot hand it back as an independent
owner. If instead the C API transferred an allocation on success, ignoring
it after making a copy may leak it. The C declaration helps locate the
producer; the kernel contract decides between these cases. Neither the
scanner that accepted the call nor the linker that resolved its symbol can
answer this last question.

